WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.939 --> 00:00:02.750
- [Narrator] Okay, just watch.

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:04.835
Three, two, one.

00:00:04.835 --> 00:00:07.779
(horn blowing)

00:00:07.779 --> 00:00:09.972
This is Thailand's Maeklong Market.

00:00:09.972 --> 00:00:12.889
(brakes squealing)

00:00:16.792 --> 00:00:18.863
About an hour south of Bangkok,

00:00:18.863 --> 00:00:21.714
every day beginning at
6:20 in the morning,

00:00:21.714 --> 00:00:24.674
a train runs through
Maeklong Railway Market,

00:00:24.674 --> 00:00:29.220
one of the largest produce and
seafood markets in Thailand,

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:33.375
through the stalls selling
fruit, ice cream, fish,

00:00:33.375 --> 00:00:35.429
through everything.

00:00:35.429 --> 00:00:36.982
And if you're wondering which came first,

00:00:36.982 --> 00:00:41.149
the market or the train,
the answer is the market.

00:00:43.217 --> 00:00:45.709
The Maeklong Railway built
a commuter train to Bangkok

00:00:45.709 --> 00:00:46.792
back in 1905.

00:00:47.929 --> 00:00:49.765
The track they laid ran
right through the middle

00:00:49.765 --> 00:00:52.717
of this market, which had
been around for decades.

00:00:52.717 --> 00:00:55.044
Rather than moving,
the vendors stayed put,

00:00:55.044 --> 00:00:57.677
adjusting their business
to the train times,

00:00:57.677 --> 00:01:00.760
eight times a day, seven days a week.

00:01:02.117 --> 00:01:04.784
(speaking Thai)

00:01:32.381 --> 00:01:34.948
- [Narrator] This system has
been perfected over the years.

00:01:34.948 --> 00:01:37.608
With produce just inches
from the train's wheels,

00:01:37.608 --> 00:01:40.711
tourists and vendors wait
as the train passes through.

00:01:40.711 --> 00:01:43.194
Then, everything goes back to normal,

00:01:43.194 --> 00:01:47.361
or at least as normal as an
active train line market can be.

